    * Resolve `named_imports` keys against the world's imports in bindgen!
    
    A `named_imports` key naming an interface that exists but isn't imported
    by the world, for example one that's only exported, panicked the macro
    since there are no import bindings to build on. This is now reported as
    an error instead.
    
    Keys are now also matched the same way as `with`: each imported interface
    uses the most specific matching key, so a key without a version applies
    to every imported version of the interface rather than just whichever was
    found first.
    
    * Use each label's own feature gate for labelled imports in bindgen!
    
    Labelled imports of the same interface share bindings and thus an
    interface id, but the world-level `add_to_linker` looked up `@unstable`
    gates by that id. Every label therefore got the gate of the first label,
    for example linking `@unstable(feature = x) import b: store;` even when
    `x` was disabled because `import a: store;` came first. Look up the
    stability of labelled imports by their label instead.
    
    * Support importing an interface both directly and under a label in bindgen!
    
    A world such as `import store; import a: store;` previously generated the
    bindings module for `store` twice, once for the plain import and once for
    the labelled import, causing a "defined multiple times" error. The same
    happened when the plain import was implicit through a `use` or came from
    an `include`, and with `with` remappings.
    
    Labelled imports now reuse the bindings of a plain import of the same
    interface regardless of import order, and the world-level `add_to_linker`
    binds the interface under its own name and under each label.
    
    Closes #14855

