    wasi: don't follow a trailing-slash symlink in `rename` (#14729)
    
    `rename` stripped a trailing slash from the source to find its parent
    directory and then re-appended it to the unresolved leaf before calling
    `renameat`. macOS and FreeBSD follow a symlink named with a trailing
    slash, so renaming `up/`, where `up` is a relative symlink to
    `../outside`, moved a directory from outside the sandbox into it.
    
    Now a trailing slash on either path requires the source to be a
    directory, on every platform. Linux never follows a symlink named with a
    trailing slash in `renameat` and fails with `ENOTDIR` instead, so there
    the slash is re-appended to the source whenever either path had one, and
    the kernel checks it atomically with the rename. Elsewhere, the source
    is checked without following symlinks and renamed by the plain names, so
    a symlink can't be followed out of the preopen. That check isn't atomic
    with the rename, but whatever replaces the source in between is still
    renamed by its plain name. Anything else fails with `ENOTDIR`, so a
    trailing slash on the destination no longer lets a file or symlink be
    renamed either.
    
    Windows previously stripped the slashes and never re-appended them, so
    `rename("file\\", "new")` and `rename("file", "new\\")` succeeded there.
    They now fail with `ERROR_DIRECTORY`, consistent with Unix.
    
    The p1, p2 and p3 tests preopen a `sandbox` directory next to an
    `outside` directory, rename a trailing-slash symlink to `../outside`
    from inside the sandbox, and check that `outside` stays in place. They
    also rename a real directory with trailing slashes, which must keep
    working. The p2 test absorbs `p2_path_rename_dir_trailing_slashes`,
    which covered those directory renames.
    
    prtest:full
    
