Author: Chris Fallin <chris@cfallin.org>
Date:   Tue Oct 13 23:43:59 2026 +0000

    Backtraces: handle stack-walking on stack overflow properly on s390x/Pulley, accounting for youngest frame. (#14687)
    
    In #14597, it was noted that on both Pulley and s390x ISAs, Wasmtime
    does not correctly handle the youngest frame where a stack overflow is
    detected.
    
    The basic difference between these two platforms and others is that on
    s390x and Pulley, the frame of the current function is not yet set up
    when the explicit stack overflow check is done; on other platforms, it
    is, because there is a small amount of slack in the limit.
    
    So on most platforms (x64, aarch64, riscv64), we can treat the
    stack-overflow trap like any other, and resolve the PC and FP at the
    trap site as indicative of function state in the bottommost frame.
    
    But on these platforms, we get a PC in the callee, and an FP still in
    the caller. We need to resolve this inconsistency by pulling either
    one or the other "up" or "down" a frame so they are consistent.
    
    I initially tried adding special "frameless PC" support so that we
    could report the actual youngest frame, without supporting any
    accesses that would use its FP (since it doesn't have a frame
    yet). This turns out to break a lot of assumptions, e.g. the ability
    to get the vmctx from a debug state slot and report what instance the
    youngest frame belongs to. In essence, it would then propagate
    complexity upward where callers would have to skip that
    special/partial frame anyway.
    
    So instead I think it is more coherent to act as if the youngest frame
    never happened: think of the stack-overflow as happening *at the
    callsite* rather than in the callee after the call succeeds. We will
    thus show a stack-overflow exception at the call instruction that
    calls a function with a big frame (or any-sized frame that happens to
    push past the limit and overflow).
    
    This does create its own kind of cross-ISA inconsistency, but IMHO
    that is more palatable, because the size of frames, and the ABI that
    implements their storage, is already an ABI- and platform-specific
    detail anyway. Basically we are saying that for our ABI on s390x and
    Pulley, because the stack limit has no slack, stack checks logically
    happen at the call rather than in the callee.
    
    This does still pass the test in #14597: no frame is "skipped", we may
    just hit overflow at a different arbitrary point on different
    platforms.
    
    Fixes #14597.

