    cranelift(x64): reuse shared i32 sums in addressing mode (#14607)
    
    * cranelift(x64): reuse shared i32 sums in addressing mode
    
    Restricts the `amode_imm_reg_iadd` lowering rule for i32 to avoid
    introducing unnecessary register pressure.
    
    Before these changes, this rule would result in the following
    transformation (the added `%shared_i32` test):
    ```
    v2 = iadd.i32 v0, v1
    v3 = iconst.i32 100
    v4 = iadd v2, v3
    return v2, v4
    ```
    lowered to
    ```
    leal (%rdi, %rsi), %eax
    leal 0x64(%rdi, %rsi), %edx
    ```
    which extends the live ranges of `v0` and `v1` despite their sum being
    available via the returned value `v2`.
    
    The updated rule reuses the shared i32 sum as the base register:
    ```
    leal (%rdi, %rsi), %eax
    leal 0x64(%rax), %edx
    ```
    
    Folding remains unrestricted for i64 sums, allowing loads/stores to use
    base+index directly.
    
    This behavior was discovered through performance analysis of a ChaCha20
    implementation where this added register pressure made a significant
    difference in throughput (measured on AMD Ryzen AI Max+ 395):
    
    | ChaCha double-round instructions | stack operations | throughput gain |
    |---:|---:|---:|
    | 163 → 141 | 52 → 37 | +7.3% |
    
