    runtime: eliminate the shared global code registry altogether in no-signals-based-traps builds. (#14690)
    
    * runtime: eliminate the shared global code registry altogether in no-signals-based-traps builds.
    
    In some high-performance embeddings of Wasmtime, the embedder may try
    to avoid all inter-thread synchronization for deterministic-latency
    reasons, and may use an `Engine` per thread. Such embedders may also
    avoid using signal handlers altogether.
    
    In a no-signals-based-traps, no-std build, Wasmtime *shouldn't* need
    the global code registry. This registry exists only to allow signal
    handlers for signals raised by the effects of trapping Wasm actions to
    find the appropriate module and trap code and take the appropriate
    action. When running without signals-based traps, the trap is instead
    implemented as a libcall, with the full `Store` accessible via an
    ordinary parameter passed to that libcall.
    
    Hence, we should be able to remove usage of the registry.
    
    This PR does so. There are some slight complications related to
    Pulley: Pulley *always* uses "signal"-based traps, where the signal is
    instead a software implementation of trapping behavior directly in the
    interpreter loop. For Pulley's case, this PR also adds a raw `Store`
    pointer to the `CallThreadState`. If that's not palatable, then I'm
    happy to figure out either how to exclude Pulley altogether from this
    "registry exemption", or refactor ownership/pointer linkages some
    other way.
    
    This PR also carries some changes to the `min-platform` example, using
    it as an integration-test for this functionality. We now do an
    additional kind of smoke-test with separate pthreads (spawned from the
    host side) that invoke a "load and instantiate this module a bunch of
    times" entry point in the no-std Wasmtime library payload, with this
    build using the no-contention-only locking stubs, ensuring that we
    would have discovered locking contention if any locking/shared
    registry access actually occurred.
    
    The `min-platform` example also needed some updating. Apparently it
    was silently failing module loads because, without virtual memory
    support, it could not make pages executable; it now does so with a
    custom code publisher. There was also a typo in an env var set from
    CI.
    
    * Code review: registry/no-registry cases factored into separate implementation submodules.
    
    * Review feedback: make Pulley go through explicit Store access as well.

