    fix: don't leak GC roots in `Table::copy` (#14627)
    
    `Table::copy` copies each element by reading it with `Table::get` and
    writing it with `Table::set`. For GC reference tables, `Table::get`
    creates a LIFO `Rooted` in the caller's current root scope. Nothing in
    `Table::copy` released those roots, so when it was called on a plain
    `&mut Store` rather than inside a `RootScope`, every copied object stayed
    rooted until the store was dropped. Repeated copies grew the root set and
    kept the GC heap from shrinking across collections.
    
    Enter a GC LIFO scope around each element's get/set pair and exit it
    afterwards, so the temporary root is released before the next element
    is copied.
    
