    pooling allocator: let an embedder release memory kept resident for idle slots (#14419)
    
    * Add `Engine::release_idle_pool_memory`
    
    `PoolingAllocationConfig::linear_memory_keep_resident` trades memory for
    page faults: after a slot is freed, up to that much of it is reset in place
    and left resident so the next instantiation does not fault it back in. That
    is the right trade while slots are reused every few milliseconds, and the
    wrong one for a slot nothing has touched in an hour — resident memory then
    follows the peak concurrency a process has ever seen rather than its current
    load, so a host sized for a daily peak carries that peak all night.
    
    Because the setting is fixed when the `Engine` is built, an embedder could
    only choose "always keep" or "never keep", never "keep it while the load
    lasts". This adds the third choice, driven by the embedder rather than by a
    timer inside Wasmtime: an embedder that already knows when it has gone idle
    can call this from that path.
    
    Nothing unique is released. The resident region holds exactly what the
    mapping restores on its own — the image for a slot that has one, written
    back by the reset, and zeros for a slot that does not — so on a platform
    whose `decommit_behavior` is `RestoreOriginalMapping` the decommit puts back
    those same contents at the cost of a fault. That is the trade the rest of
    the slot already makes.
    
    Slots are taken out of the free lists before their memory is released and
    returned afterwards, so nothing can allocate one while it is being
    decommitted. That is the order `DecommitQueue::flush` already uses. Each
    shard is locked in turn rather than all at once, and the `madvise` happens
    outside the lock.
    
    A failed decommit leaves the pages resident with the contents they had,
    which is a correct state: those slots go back reporting the same bytes they
    did before and nothing is lost but the reclaim.
    
    The test covers both halves — that the bytes are actually released, and
    that the next instantiation still reads what its module says it should.
    
    * Keep a slot's module affinity when taking it to release memory
    
    `free` reads the module back out of the `Used` payload to re-file the slot
    on its module's affine list, so marking a taken slot `Used(None)` un-affined
    every slot this touched. The next instantiation of that module could then
    land on a different slot and remap its image — a call made to reclaim memory
    quietly costing image reuse.
    
    The test fails without the fix.
    
    * Remove a duplicated `#[test]` attribute
    
    Left behind when the affinity test was inserted above this one. It
    registered `test_affinity_allocation_strategy` twice and tripped
    `duplicate_macro_attributes`, which this repository denies.
    
    * Address review: tighten the comments
    
    - Restore `unused_bytes_resident`'s doc-comment, which the new method's
      insertion had taken over, and put the new method above it.
    - Rewrite the `MemoryPool` and `Engine` doc-comments along the lines cfallin
      suggested: what the method does and the tradeoff, without restating the
      rationale for the feature on every item.
    - Plain ASCII in the doc-comments.
