    Alias analysis: Reuse the exit state of a block's single predecessor (#14425)
    
    Following the same existing logic we use for determining memory "extents", if a
    block has a single predecessor, then entering it sees the same memory state that the
    single predecessor exited with. By reusing the single predecessor's exit state,
    we get strictly more-precise analysis results because the fixpoint's initial
    block entry states were computed on the function before optimization, and
    therefore can include dead stores that we have since eliminated, while the
    single predecessor's exit state reflects the post-optimization state.
    
    This shaves off another ~13% of time from the increment-each-byte compile-time
    builtins benchmark. See the associated disas test output updates: inside the
    loop body, another redundant load was eliminated, which cascaded into another
    `trapz` getting deduplicated by GVN.
    
    On Sightglass, this actually speeds up compilation, shaving off 0.57% of
    instructions retired across the whole suite. It doesn't make any statistically
    significant difference to execution on Sightglass.

