    pooling allocator: complete the pagemap scan past 32 regions, and reset dirty pages that are not present (#14357)
    
    * pooling allocator: complete the pagemap scan past 32 regions, and reset dirty pages that are not present
    
    `reset_with_pagemap` collected dirty regions into a stack buffer of 32 and
    treated the kernel's `walk_end` as the end of the memory it would ever
    reset in place: everything after it was decommitted. A guest whose heap
    dirties more than 32 disjoint runs therefore had most of its memory
    decommitted on every reset and refaulted it on the next instantiation
    (95-315 minor faults per request measured with a QuickJS guest), and the
    madvise/mprotect storm capped multi-core scaling with TLB-shootdown IPIs.
    
    The scan now resumes from `walk_end` whenever the buffer fills, and stops
    early only when the `keep_resident` page budget is spent, which is what
    `walk_end` is for. The buffer stays on the stack (64 regions); the loop
    costs one ioctl per 64 regions and no allocation.
    
    The category mask required `PRESENT`. A dirty page the kernel has swapped
    out is `WRITTEN | SWAPPED`, not `PRESENT`: it was neither reset in place
    nor decommitted (it sits before `walk_end`), so the next instance in the
    slot read the previous instance's bytes. Reproduced with `MADV_PAGEOUT`
    between two instantiations of one slot. The mask now requires `WRITTEN`
    and not `PFNZERO`/`FILE` regardless of residency; resetting a swapped page
    pages it in and overwrites it. With 32 regions the accidental decommit of
    everything after `walk_end` hid most of this; with a complete traversal it
    would be exposed on every host with swap, so the two changes belong
    together.
    
    Unit tests (skipped where PAGEMAP_SCAN is unavailable): a 400-page mapping
    with 200 disjoint dirty pages is fully reset in place with nothing
    decommitted; the same mapping with a 100-page budget resets exactly 100 and
    decommits the rest; 64 dirty pages paged out with MADV_PAGEOUT are all
    reset. All three fail on the previous implementation (32 pages reset; 0 of
    64 paged-out pages reset on a host with swap).
    
    * pagemap tests: no nested unsafe in the decommit closure (clippy: unnecessary_unsafe)
    
    * pagemap: review fixes — drop the stray .orig file, update the scan's documented criteria, one budget local
    
    - Remove `pagemap.rs.orig`, committed by accident.
    - The search criteria no longer list `PRESENT`; the `WRITTEN` bullet says
      why a swapped-out dirty page must still be reset, replacing the separate
      paragraph below it.
    - `remaining_budget` and `pages_found` become one `budget` local that the
      reported regions spend, and the loop condition carries the "budget left"
      test.
    
