    component: improve async-mismatch diagnostics and stub async imports (#13821)
    
    Fixes #13813.
    
    `func_new`/`func_wrap` and `func_new_async`/`func_wrap_async` all construct
    a `DynamicHostFn`/`StaticHostFn` with `ASYNC = false` - only
    `func_new_concurrent`/`func_wrap_concurrent` construct one with
    `ASYNC = true`. Trying to satisfy an `async func`-typed import with the
    former (an easy mistake, since `func_new_async`'s name suggests it should
    work) always failed at instantiation with a bare "type mismatch with
    async", giving no indication of why or what to use instead.
    
    Two changes:
    
    1. `typecheck_async` (new, shared between `StaticHostFn`/`DynamicHostFn`)
       names the specific mismatch and points at the correct API:
       - before: `type mismatch with async`
       - after:  `type mismatch with async: this import is declared \`async
         func\` in WIT, but was satisfied with a sync-style host function
         (\`func_new\`/\`func_wrap\`, or \`func_new_async\`/\`func_wrap_async\`
         — despite the name, these implement a *sync*-WIT-typed function via
         blocking host code, not an \`async func\` import); use
         \`func_new_concurrent\`/\`func_wrap_concurrent\` instead`
       (and the symmetric message for the reverse mismatch.)
    
    2. `Linker::define_unknown_imports_as_traps` always stubbed with
       `func_new`, so it could never stub an unsatisfied `async func` import at
       all (not even as a trap) - a component with one couldn't be instantiated
       even if the import was never called. It now stubs with
       `func_new_concurrent` when the import is async and
       `Config::concurrency_support` is enabled, falling back to `func_new`
       (and the improved error above) otherwise.
    
    Tested against the exact repro from the issue (a dwarf-built component
    importing `async func consume(x: u32) -> string`) - before: instantiation
    fails immediately, `define_unknown_imports_as_traps`'s stub is silently
    useless; after: the stub is created, and calling `probe()` (which calls
    `consume`) traps with a clear "unknown import ... has not been defined"
    instead of failing at instantiation.
    
