    * Refine async/store bindgen flags
    
    This commit refactors the implementation of `bindgen!` to refine the
    interpretation of a WIT function's type in combination with specified
    flags (e.g. `store | async`) during bindings generation. This system
    originated when `async` wasn't part of WIT function types and hasn't
    been fully reconciled with adding `async` to WIT function types. Notably
    support for `ignore-wit` does not make sense because if the WIT is
    `async` then the function must use `func_wrap_concurrent` and nothing
    else.
    
    The specific change made in this commit is to reinterpret the `store |
    async` flags on a synchronous WIT function. Previously this would use
    `func_wrap_concurrent` which would actually mean that the WIT function
    was incorrectly bound (it'd be typed as `async` on the host but guests
    would import it as a sync function). Instead now `store | async` is
    interpreted as "Rust-level `async`" combined with "takes an `Access`".
    This required some refactoring internally in bindgen to ensure this is
    handled correctly and mostly involves separating out the concurrent case
    explicitly based on the function type and then handling the sync/async
    flags.
    
    * Consult the host's `socket_addr_check` for implicit binds
    
    This commit is an update to the WASIp3 implementation of TCP/UDP to let
    the host have an opinion about implicit binds performed in the WASIp3
    APIs. The primary bug this fixes is that if networking is disallowed
    (e.g. `-Sinherit-network` isn't passed on the CLI) then it's actually
    still possible to call `listen()` on a TCP socket as the limiting
    mechanism, address checks, was never previously invoked. The fixes in
    this commit are to lift the implicit bind operations to performing a
    socket address check and then doing an implicit bind.
    
